#. type: Title #
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:1
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Closing an event"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:4
#, markdown-text
msgid "While an event runs, everything in it can still change: a result is corrected, a registration moves, a programme gets a new price. At some point the organizer has to say *this is the result*. That is what **Close event** does: it turns a running event into a finished one."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What closing means {#meaning}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid "Closing does two things at once:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:11
#, markdown-text
msgid "**The rankings become final and are released.** Until now they were a state of work; from now on they are the result of the event."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:11
#, markdown-text
msgid "**The event becomes read-only.** Registrations, entries, results, awards, programmes with their series, prices and award tiers, classifications and the event's settings can no longer be changed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:13
#, markdown-text
msgid "Everything stays visible: the dashboard, the results, the rankings, the statistics, the reports and the documents. A banner on every page of the event says that it is closed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:15
#, markdown-text
msgid "The two belong together. A ranking can only be final if nothing beneath it can change any more, and Standblatt enforces this in the database itself, not only by hiding buttons: every change to the data of a closed event is refused, whether it comes from the app, a helper link or an API token."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:16
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why closed events are read-only {#read-only}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:19
#, markdown-text
msgid "Once an event is closed, other things rely on it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:23
#, markdown-text
msgid "Shooters copy their results and ranks into their personal training logs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:23
#, markdown-text
msgid "The financial report, the awards and the club competition have been handed out and settled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:23
#, markdown-text
msgid "Next year's event may have been copied from it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:25
#, markdown-text
msgid "If results could still change, a shooter's log could show a rank the ranking no longer has, and the reports would no longer match what was paid and handed out. That is also why things that happen at the desk, such as marking awards as handed out, have to be done before closing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:26
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The link to My training {#my-training}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:29
#, markdown-text
msgid "Every Standblatt user has a personal training log, [My training](../my-training/index.md). A shooter who enters a **licence number** and a **birthday** in the profile is matched with the shooters of every club: wherever a club's master data has a shooter with the same licence number (leading zeros do not matter) and the same birthday, that shooter's results belong to the user."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:31
#, markdown-text
msgid "Only results of **closed** events are offered, because only their ranking is final. Each one comes with the rank in its programme and the number of participants, e.g. *Rank 12 of 87*. Nothing is added by itself: the shooter chooses a discipline and adds the result, and it becomes a competition session of the log with its series and its rank."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:33
#, markdown-text
msgid "The match works across clubs without any club giving up its data: the clubs' master data stays where it is, the match only reads it for the user it belongs to, and only the shooter's own results are shown. A shooter without a licence number or a birthday in the profile, or whose club recorded a different birthday, gets no competition results. See [Profile and competition results](../my-training/account.md#competitions)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:34
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reopening {#reopen}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Reopen** makes a closed event editable again, e.g. when a wrong result shows up after closing. It is meant for corrections, not as a second state of work:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:40
#, markdown-text
msgid "Results that shooters already added to their logs stay there as they were added; a correction afterwards does not reach them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:40
#, markdown-text
msgid "Results not added yet are no longer offered in *My training* until the event is closed again."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:42
#, markdown-text
msgid "Close the event again once the correction is done."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/closing.md:43
#, markdown-text
msgid "A closed event cannot be deleted; reopen it first. It can be copied for next year while it is closed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:1
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clubs, accounts and data"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:4
#, markdown-text
msgid "Standblatt serves many clubs on one server. This page explains who owns which data, why people have one account for everything, and why the shooters of one club are not those of another."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clubs are tenants {#tenants}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid "A **club** in Standblatt is a tenant: its ranges, shooters, clubs list, events, programmes, print forms and users belong to it and to nobody else. Clubs never see each other's data, not even that a shooter or an event exists elsewhere. The separation is part of every query the server makes, not a filter of the interface."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:9
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "One account, a role per club {#accounts}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid "People are different from data: the same person can be the secretary of one club, help at the desk of another club's festival and keep a personal training log. Standblatt therefore has **global accounts**. One account logs in once and reaches every club it belongs to, with a role per club that decides what it may do there (see [Roles](../user-guide/users.md#roles))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:14
#, markdown-text
msgid "A club does not own the account, only the membership. Removing a member takes away the access to that club; the account, its training log and its other clubs stay. Clubs invite people by email, and the person becomes a member only by accepting the link, so nobody is added to a club without knowing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:16
#, markdown-text
msgid "Helpers at an event do not need an account at all: a [helper link](../user-guide/helpers.md) gives access to one event and one task. An [API token](../user-guide/login-language.md#api-tokens) acts for its owner and never with more than the owner's role in a club."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:17
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shooter master data per club {#shooters}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:20
#, markdown-text
msgid "A **shooter** is not an account. It is a record in a club's master data: name, licence number, birthday, address and the clubs the shooter belongs to. Each club keeps its own shooters, imported from a CSV file, typed at the desk, or taken over from an online booking."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:22
#, markdown-text
msgid "There is no shared register of all licensed shooters: the federation's member data is not part of Standblatt, and since clubs never see each other's data, the shooters one club entered stay invisible to the others. The price is that a shooter who shoots at three clubs' events is recorded three times, once in each club. Where the records have to come together, for a shooter's own training log, the licence number and the birthday do it (see [Closing an event](closing.md#my-training))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:23
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The shooter's club and the tenant club {#shooter-club}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:26
#, markdown-text
msgid "Two different things are called a club:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:29
#, markdown-text
msgid "The **tenant club** is the club that uses Standblatt and runs the event."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:29
#, markdown-text
msgid "The **shooter's club** is an entry in the tenant's **Clubs** list: the club a shooter belongs to, e.g. a guest club at a shooting festival. These clubs have no access to Standblatt; they are names with an SSV number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:31
#, markdown-text
msgid "A shooter can belong to several clubs and registers for one of them. That club is printed on the documents and counts in the rankings and the club competition. Because clubs are matched by name regardless of upper case and spaces, and new ones are created only on purpose, a typo does not turn one club into two; where it happened anyway, merging cleans it up, even in closed events."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:32
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Live updates {#live}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:35
#, markdown-text
msgid "Several people work on the same event at once: the desk registers, scorers enter results at the lanes, kiosk screens show the rankings in the hall. Whenever something is saved, the server tells every open page of that event, and each page fetches what changed. A result entered at a lane appears on the dashboard and the kiosk screens without reloading. After a network interruption a page catches up by itself, so nothing is missed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:36
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Offline {#offline}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:39
#, markdown-text
msgid "Club work needs a connection. A lane can only be given once, a shooter can shoot a programme only once, and a result saved on one device must be on all the others; only the server can decide that for everybody at the same time. Without a connection, club pages and helper links show a red bar and save nothing, but open dialogs keep what was typed, so it can be saved once the connection is back."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/data.md:40
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "*My training* is different, because nobody else works on a personal log. A session saved without a connection is kept on the device and uploaded later, without ever creating it twice, and the installed app opens without a connection with the last data. See [Unfinished sessions and offline](../my-training/logging.md#unfinished-sessions-and-offline).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:1
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules and kinds of events"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:4
#, markdown-text
msgid "Clubs run very different events: a shooting festival with a desk, lane assignment and wreath cards, a Feldschiessen on the official weekend, an annual programme shot over the season, a club championship. Standblatt has no separate code for each of them. Every event is the same kind of thing; events differ only in their programmes, their ranges and days, and the **modules** they switch on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The core and the modules {#core}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid "Every event has the same core: name, days, ranges and language, programmes with their series and prices, registrations, results, rankings, statistics and closing. Everything beyond that is a module: lane assignment, reservations with the online booking, the lane sheet and the control strip, the ammunition desk, attempts, awards, special prizes, the club competition, classifications, kiosk displays and helper links."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid "A module that is off is gone, not only hidden: it disappears from the sidebar, the dashboard, the programme editor, the rankings and the reports, and the server refuses its functions too, so neither a helper link nor an API token can use it. An annual programme without awards therefore has no award section in its programmes and no award reports; nobody has to skip past what the event does not use."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid "Some modules need others: *Special prizes* switch on *Awards*, because a special prize is an award, and *Reservations* need *Lane assignment*, because a reservation blocks lanes. A module stays on as long as a module that needs it is on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:13
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Kinds of events are only a starting point {#kinds}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:16
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**New event** asks what kind of event it is: shooting festival, annual programme or training, Feldschiessen, club championship or a custom selection. The kind only switches on a set of modules (the list is under [Event kinds](../reference/modules.md#kinds)); the event does not remember it. Afterwards, the modules can be changed like those of any other event, so a Feldschiessen that also wants classifications switches them on, and a shooting festival without a desk switches lane assignment off.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid "This is why a new kind of event needs no new feature: if a combination of modules covers it, it can be run today. A club that holds an event every year in the same form saves it as a [template](../user-guide/events.md#templates) instead; a template is an event with modules, programmes and classifications, not a kind."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:19
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why a module that holds data stays on {#in-use}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:22
#, markdown-text
msgid "Switching off a module that holds data would hide data that still counts: award tiers that shooters have reached, kiosk links that still show the rankings in the hall, programmes that allow several attempts. Standblatt therefore keeps such a module on until its data is gone, and the module card says why its switch is locked:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:33
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| Module | Stays on while |\n|---|---|\n| Lane assignment | a registration has a lane |\n| Reservations | the online booking link is active |\n| Attempts | a programme allows more than one attempt |\n| Awards | a programme has award tiers |\n| Special prizes | a programme awards special prizes |\n| Classifications | the event has a classification |\n| Kiosk displays | the event has a kiosk link |\n| Helper links | the event has a helper link |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:35
#, markdown-text
msgid "The lane sheet, the control strip, the ammunition desk and the club competition hold no data of their own and can be switched at any time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:36
#: ../src/help/en/user-guide/events.md:7
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Configuration and operation {#configuration}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:39
#, markdown-text
msgid "An event is prepared once and run many times: the programmes, prices and modules are set up before the first shooter arrives, while the desk works on the day. Standblatt keeps the two apart. The **operation** (dashboard, results, rankings, statistics and the pages of modules such as reservations) needs the permission *Run events*; the **configuration** (general settings, modules, programmes, the set-up pages of modules, copying and closing) needs *Configure events*. A helper at the desk cannot change a price by accident, and the configuration is not in the way of the desk."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:40
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Club modules and event modules {#club-modules}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:43
#, markdown-text
msgid "Most modules belong to an event, because each event decides whether it hands out awards or uses kiosk screens. Some building blocks are used by every event of a club and therefore belong to the club: the **print forms** with their image library are drawn once and used by the programmes of every event. Such **club modules** are switched under [Club modules](../user-guide/events.md#club-modules). A new club has print forms switched on; like event modules, the module stays on while the club has forms or images."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:44
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Events with and without lanes {#lanes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:47
#, markdown-text
msgid "Lanes matter only where a desk assigns them. Standblatt therefore separates the range from the lanes:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:53
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| The event has | What follows |\n|---|---|\n| a range and *Lane assignment* | the desk of a shooting festival: lane grid, reservations, online booking, scorer links and lane status on kiosk screens |\n| a range, but no *Lane assignment* | shooters are registered without a lane; documents still go to the range's print agent and programmes can still be tied to the range |\n| no range | no lanes and nothing that needs them; documents open as PDF, because print agents belong to a range |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:55
#, markdown-text
msgid "An annual programme or a Feldschiessen whose results come on paper or from the scoring system needs no lanes, but the club may still want its printer at the range; that is the middle row. Modules that need lanes are switched off by themselves when the event has no range."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:57
#, markdown-text
msgid "Results reach an event without lanes the same way as any other: through **Enter results**, a CSV file or the results of a registration (see [Enter results](../user-guide/results-entry.md))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:58
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copying keeps the shape {#copy}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/modules.md:60
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Copy for next year** and templates take over the modules that are switched on and those that hold data, together with the programmes and classifications. An event copied from last year therefore starts with the same shape, without its registrations and results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:1
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How printing works"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:4
#, markdown-text
msgid "A shooting festival prints a lot: a lane sheet and a control strip for every registration, result sheets, wreath cards, forms. Standblatt is a web app, and that shapes how printing works."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every document is a PDF {#pdf}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid "The server renders every document as a PDF, in the language of the event: lane sheets, control strips, result sheets, wreath cards, print forms, certificates and rankings. The same PDF is printed at the range, opened in the browser or shared from a phone, so a document looks the same wherever it comes out. Rankings are only downloaded, as PDF or CSV, and never sent to a printer of the range."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:9
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why a print agent {#agent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid "A browser cannot print on its own: every print asks for a dialog, a printer and a click. At a busy desk that is too slow, and a lane sheet should come out of the printer next to the desk while the control strip comes out of the label printer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:14
#, markdown-text
msgid "The **print agent** solves this. It is a small program on a computer next to the printers: the Standblatt Print Agent app on Windows or a Mac, or a headless agent on a Linux computer such as a Raspberry Pi. When someone presses a Print button, the server puts the PDF into a queue; the agent fetches it and prints it on the chosen printer without asking. The agent connects to Standblatt, not the other way round, so nothing at the range has to be reachable from the internet."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:16
#, markdown-text
msgid "Each print job is confirmed by the agent. A job that is not confirmed within two minutes is handed out again, up to three attempts; **Recent jobs** shows what happened."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:17
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Agents and printers belong to a range {#range}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:20
#, markdown-text
msgid "Printers stand at a range, not in an event, so the print agent and the printer settings belong to the [range](../getting-started/concepts.md#ranges-and-lanes). Every event on that range uses them without any set-up of its own. A registration's documents print on the range of its lane; an event on several ranges therefore prints at the range where the shooter shoots. One computer can serve several ranges, e.g. *Pistol 25 m* and *Pistol 50 m* in the same hall."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:22
#, markdown-text
msgid "The settings choose a printer **per document**, because the documents need different printers: the control strip goes to a label printer, the lane sheet to an A5 tray, wreath cards and print forms to a manual feed with the pre-printed sheets. The printer options the agent reports (tray, paper size, two-sided and so on) are stored with the choice."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:24
#, markdown-text
msgid "An agent is connected either with a token that is copied into its settings or, with the app, by a code shown on the computer and confirmed in Standblatt (see [Printing](../user-guide/printing.md#connect)). Either way the server keeps only a fingerprint of the token, never the token itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:25
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When there is no printer {#fallback}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:28
#, markdown-text
msgid "A document goes to the printer only if its range has a printer chosen for that document and an agent of the range was online within the last minute. Otherwise the PDF opens instead: in a new tab on a computer, in the share sheet on a phone or tablet (print, save to files, send), so the app stays open. Nothing is lost when the agent is off; the desk just prints by hand."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:30
#, markdown-text
msgid "The same holds for events without a range: there is no agent to send to, so every document opens as PDF."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:31
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Print forms on pre-printed sheets {#forms}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:34
#, markdown-text
msgid "Some documents are not designed by Standblatt but supplied on paper: wreath cards, the lane sheets the army supplies for the obligatory programme, a club's own certificates. A **print form** describes such a sheet: where on the paper each value goes, in millimetres from the top left corner. Printing a form puts only the values onto the sheet that is already in the printer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:36
#, markdown-text
msgid "A form has a **data source**: the results of a programme, a registration or a rank. The source decides which values the form can print and where it is used: forms of results are printed after a programme is completed or as wreath cards, forms of a registration as control strips or participant cards, forms of a rank as certificates from the rankings. The wreath card and the control strip are themselves built-in forms that a club can copy and adjust."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:38
#, markdown-text
msgid "A scan of the empty sheet can lie behind the form in the editor, so the fields can be placed onto its boxes. The scan is for placing only and is never printed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:39
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Calibration in principle {#calibration}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/printing.md:41
#, markdown-text
msgid "Printers rarely print exactly where they should: paper feeds and trays shift the print by a millimetre or two. A form that is right on screen can therefore land beside the boxes on paper. The **printer offset** of the form moves every element by the same amount to make up for it: print a test sheet, measure how far the values are off and enter the difference. The offset is part of the form, so a form printed on another printer that shifts differently needs a copy with its own offset. The preview ignores the offset and shows the form as designed. The steps are in [Print results onto the army's lane sheets](../how-to/army-sheet.md#calibrate)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:1
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How rankings fit together"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:4
#, markdown-text
msgid "An event can rank its shooters in several ways at once: per programme, over several programmes or attempts, by club, and by age category for special prizes. They are not separate competitions with separate data. All of them start from the same results, and most of them start from the same programme ranking."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The programme ranking {#programme}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid "The programme ranking is the base. Every programme has one ranking of everybody who shot it, ordered by the [SSV rules](../reference/rules.md#ranking): total, then the series from the last one backwards, then mouches, then age. There is only one implementation of this order, and it feeds the rankings page, the kiosk screens, the ranking PDF and CSV, the special prizes and the rank a shooter sees in *My training*. A shooter's place is therefore the same wherever it appears."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid "Ranks are consecutive (1, 2, 3, … even on equal results): the tie-break decides almost every tie, so two shooters never share a rank."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:12
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Variants** are separate programmes with the same name, for example *Sport* and *Supported*, and each one has its own ranking: shooting supported and shooting freehand are not compared.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:14
#, markdown-text
msgid "A **combined** programme takes series from another programme instead of being shot separately. Its results are copies of the picked series of its source, so it is ranked like any other programme; when the source changes, the copy follows, and with it the ranking."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:15
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Attempts and the counting attempt {#attempts}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid "A programme that allows several attempts keeps every attempt as an entry with its own results. A ranking, however, lists each shooter once. The programme's **counting attempt** (the best, the first or the last) decides which attempt stands for the shooter, and the ranking shows which one it is, e.g. *2/3*."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:20
#, markdown-text
msgid "The same counting attempt is used wherever one result per shooter is needed: the ranking, the club competition, the special prizes and the rank in *My training*. Awards are different, because an award belongs to a result rather than to a ranking: every attempt can earn its own award, unless *Only the counting attempt receives an award* is on, in which case the award moves to whichever attempt counts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:22
#, markdown-text
msgid "Combinations take the series of one result; with several attempts it would be unclear which one, so programmes with several attempts cannot be combined."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:23
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Classifications {#classifications}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:26
#, markdown-text
msgid "A classification ranks shooters over several programmes or over several attempts of one programme: the sum of three programmes for a section championship, the best three attempts of an annual programme for a club championship. It takes the counting attempts of each programme, adds up what counts, may count only the best programmes, and ranks with its own tie-break and its own classes of age categories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:28
#, markdown-text
msgid "A classification only reads results. It never changes a result, an award or a programme ranking, and an event can have several classifications next to its programme rankings. The programme ranking itself can be seen as the simplest classification: one programme, one attempt, the SSV tie-break and no classes. See [Classifications](../user-guide/classifications.md) for the settings."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:29
#: ../src/help/en/reference/rules.md:60
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Club competition {#club-competition}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:32
#, markdown-text
msgid "The club competition ranks clubs, not shooters. It uses only programmes marked *Counts for the club competition* and only completed results of shooters who registered for a club; the club is the one chosen at registration, not the tenant club. Each result scores with its first two series, and a club's result is the average of its best results, with a small bonus for every further participant (see [Club competition](../reference/rules.md#club-competition)). Because it has its own score, the club competition does not follow the programme ranking."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:33
#: ../src/help/en/reference/rules.md:71
#: ../src/help/en/user-guide/programmes.md:51
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Special prizes {#special-prizes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:36
#, markdown-text
msgid "Special prizes go to the best ranks of every age category. They do follow the programme ranking: they walk down it per age category and skip shooters whose club or category has too few participants (see [Special prizes](../reference/rules.md#special-prizes)). Their settings can be changed while the event runs, because they only read the ranking."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Which ranking uses what {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:45
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| Ranking | Ranks | From | Counting attempt | Order |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Programme ranking | shooters | one programme | yes | SSV rules |\n| Classification | shooters, per class | several programmes or attempts | its own number per programme | own tie-break, SSV by default |\n| Club competition | clubs | programmes counting for the club competition | yes | club result |\n| Special prizes | shooters, per age category | the programme ranking | yes | the programme ranking |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:47
#, markdown-text
msgid "Awards are not a ranking: a shooter reaches an award with their own total in their age category, however the others shoot."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:48
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why the series are locked {#locked}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:51
#, markdown-text
msgid "Results are stored per series. Once shooters are registered for a programme, a change to the number of series, their shots, their maximum or whether they track mouches or single shots would change what the stored results mean: a total of 96 out of 100 is not a total out of 200, and every ranking that compares them would be wrong. Standblatt therefore fixes the series as soon as a shooter is registered."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:53
#, markdown-text
msgid "Everything that does not change the meaning of a result stays open: names, prices, award values and limits, the flags. Age categories and award tiers can be added but not removed, because registrations were priced and awards were given by them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:54
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Live and final {#live}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/explanation/rankings.md:56
#, markdown-text
msgid "All rankings are computed from the results whenever they are shown, so a saved result appears at once on the rankings page and the kiosk screens. While the event runs they can still change. [Closing](closing.md) the event makes them final."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:1
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Concepts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:4
#, markdown-text
msgid "This page explains the words Standblatt uses and how they fit together. The German, French and Italian terms are in the [Glossary](../reference/glossary.md); the steps for each task are in the user guide."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:6
#, markdown-text
msgid "In short: a **club** has **ranges**, **shooters** and **users**. It runs **events**; an event offers **programmes** and switches on the **modules** it needs. A **registration** puts a shooter on the event with one or more programmes, each programme becomes an **entry**, the entry gets **results**, and the results give **awards** and a place in the **rankings**. When the event is **closed**, the rankings are final and reach the shooters' personal training logs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:7
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clubs, users and roles {#clubs-users-and-roles}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid "A **club** is a tenant: everything in Standblatt belongs to exactly one club, and clubs never see each other's data. Only the platform operator creates clubs, in the admin panel. Accounts come from an invitation of a club, from the sign-up of [My training](../my-training/account.md), from **Add user** on a club's users page or from the operator."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:12
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Users** are personal accounts. One account can belong to several clubs, with one role per club. A role is a set of permissions such as *Run events* or *Configure events*; every club starts with the roles *Admin*, *Board*, *Helper* and *Member* and can change them or add its own (see [Roles](../user-guide/users.md#roles)). Clubs invite people by email; the invited person joins by accepting the link.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:14
#, markdown-text
msgid "Why accounts are global while all club data stays in the club is explained in [Clubs, accounts and data](../explanation/data.md)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:15
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ranges and lanes {#ranges-and-lanes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid "A **range** (German *Stand*) is a shooting range of the club with a number of **lanes** (*Scheiben*). Lanes can be marked out of service. Printers and the print agent belong to a range."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:20
#, markdown-text
msgid "Each distance is a range of its own: a hall with 25 m and 50 m lanes is two ranges, each with lanes from 1. An event can be shot on several ranges, on one, or on none at all (see [Events with and without lanes](../explanation/modules.md#lanes))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:21
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Events and programmes {#events-and-programmes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:24
#, markdown-text
msgid "An **event** (*Anlass*) is a competition on one or more ranges on one or more days (the date is the main day; further days cover pre-shooting and multi-day events). It has a language, which is used for printed documents and kiosk screens, and [modules](../user-guide/events.md#modules) that switch on what it needs, such as lane assignment, awards or the club competition."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:26
#, markdown-text
msgid "There are no event types: a shooting festival, a Feldschiessen and an annual programme are the same kind of event with different modules (see [Modules and kinds of events](../explanation/modules.md))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:28
#, markdown-text
msgid "An event offers **programmes** (*Stiche*): each programme has **series** (*Passen*) with a number of shots and maximum points, **prices** per age category and **award tiers** (wreath cards, prizes in kind or cash prizes) with a minimum total per age category. Programmes with the same name are **variants** (for example *Sport* and *Supported*); a shooter shoots one variant. A programme can be **combinable** with another programme: it then takes some of its series from that programme instead of being shot separately."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:30
#, markdown-text
msgid "A programme can allow several **attempts**, of which one counts. A **classification** ranks shooters over several programmes or attempts. How programme rankings, attempts, classifications, the club competition and special prizes relate is explained in [How rankings fit together](../explanation/rankings.md)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:31
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shooters, registrations and entries {#shooters-registrations-and-entries}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:34
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Shooters** are the people who shoot. Each club keeps its own shooter master data, together with the **clubs** shooters belong to (the shooter's own club, not to be confused with the tenant club).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:36
#, markdown-text
msgid "A **registration** puts a shooter on the event, optionally on a lane, with one or more programmes. Each programme of a registration is an **entry**. A registration is *registered* (waiting), *shooting* (on a lane) or *completed* (all programmes have results; the lane is free again)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Results, awards and rankings {#results-awards-and-rankings}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:40
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Results** are the points per series, optionally with mouches (inner tens). Once all series are in, the shooter may receive one of the **awards** the total reaches in the shooter's age category. **Rankings** order all results of a programme by the [SSV rules](../reference/rules.md#ranking).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:42
#, markdown-text
msgid "An award depends only on the shooter's own total; a rank depends on everybody else's. Rankings are live while the event runs and become final when it is [closed](../explanation/closing.md)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:43
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Age categories {#age-categories}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:46
#, markdown-text
msgid "The age category follows the SSV rule: the year of the event minus the year of birth. The day of birth does not matter, and the year is that of the event's main day, so a pre-shooting day in December counts for a January event. The category is fixed when the shooter registers and decides the prices, awards and special prizes the shooter can get. A shooter without a birthday counts as Elite. The categories and their ages are listed in [Competition rules](../reference/rules.md#age-categories)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:47
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Documents and printing {#documents-and-printing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../src/help/en/getting-started/concepts.md:49
#, markdown-text
msgid "Every document is a PDF rendered by the server in the event's language. At the range, a small print agent sends it to the right printer; without one, the PDF opens in the browser. See [How printing works](../explanation/printing.md)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
